#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""orionx-postured — make the threat-posture tier mean something.

@decision DEC-PHASE12-024
@title Threat posture is enforced by a daemon, and a blind IDS says so out loud
@status accepted
@rationale W10-5 shipped the tier selector (DEC-PHASE10-005) and W11-6 Layer A
  shipped Suricata behind a lazy-start gate (DEC-PHASE11-008). Nothing joined
  them. Selecting "Tier 2 · Deception" wrote a '2' to
  ~/.config/orionx/threat-posture, recoloured one Cockpit badge, and changed
  nothing else: `nmap -Pn -A` against the deck at Tier 2 produced no Suricata
  output at all. Three independent reasons, all real:

    1. Nothing started suricata.service when the tier was raised.
    2. Its drop-in sets ConditionPathExists=/var/lib/suricata/orionx-enabled
       and that file never existed, so systemd skipped the unit and reported
       success while doing nothing.
    3. /var/lib/suricata/rules is empty. The 22 files in /etc/suricata/rules
       are Debian's stock decoder/stream protocol-anomaly rules, not threat
       signatures. ET-Open arrives only via `orionx-freshen-suricata`, which
       needs a network the deck may not have.

  This daemon closes all three, and the third one is the reason it exists.
  A detector that is installed, enabled, running, and carrying no signatures
  reports nothing — which is indistinguishable, from the operator's chair,
  from a quiet network. That is the failure mode that made the deck look like
  it was watching when it was not. So: SILENT NON-DETECTION IS A BUG. Whenever
  a tier that implies IDS coverage is active without usable threat rules, this
  daemon says so on the bus, at critical, naming the remedy, and keeps saying
  it. It would rather be annoying than dishonest.

  Design constraints that shaped it:

  - Air-gap first. stdlib only, no pip, no network lookups. The ATT&CK map is
    a static local table; the Nebula call is to 127.0.0.1.
  - The model never gates an alert. Every alert is published BEFORE it is
    queued for contextualisation, on a bounded queue, from a worker thread.
    If ollama is absent, slow, or wedged, detection is unaffected.
  - No second scan detector. orionx-scanwatch (DEC-PHASE12-022) already reports
    port scans from the firewall's own drop log at Tier 0 and needs no rules.
    Suricata's scan-class alerts for a source scanwatch just reported are
    suppressed, so one scan is one story, not two.
  - Deception is local and non-destructive: listening decoys and canary files
    on this host. It never probes, scans, or sends anything to another host.
    Tier 2 is the explicit operator opt-in DEC-PHASE9-008 requires.
  - Runtime state only. The decoy firewall openings live in their own nft
    table created at arm time and destroyed at disarm; /etc/nftables.conf
    remains the single authority for base policy and is never edited.

Usage:
    orionx-postured                       # daemon (systemd)
    orionx-postured --once --dry-run      # apply nothing, print the plan
    orionx-postured --print-plan          # JSON plan for the current tier
"""
